rule SEC103 "cors-allows-any-origin" {
  lang python
  severity error

  find c: call where c.callee.name == "CORS" and c.text ~ /origins?\s*=\s*(\[\s*)?["']\*["']/
  report c "CORS is configured to allow any origin"

  example fire python """
    CORS(app, origins="*")  #~ error
  """
  example clean python """
    CORS(app, origins=["https://app.example.com"])
  """
  example notapplicable markdown """
    Prose configures no CORS.
  """
  explain """
    A wildcard origin lets every site read responses from the browser, and combined with credentials it hands session-bound data to any page the user visits (WEBSEC, registered in the SEC namespace). The check reads flask-cors style `CORS(...)` calls; other frameworks and languages need an adapter and are reported Unresolved by the family, never clean.
    ## Remedy
    List the exact origins that need access, read from configuration that differs per environment.
  """
}
